ately was was  something of a diary in all of his music  and I would just I went to all of his  lyrics I thought he's writing everything  straight from his heart straight from  his soul so I've started writing  everything out and you know compiled  what I thought was you know a story of  his life and and the things that he was  extremely passionate about a thing that  things that he was longing for and you  see a few themes running through which  are loneliness and the search for love  and trying to fill a certain void Lucy  I'm so fascinated by the relationship as  portrayed in the film between Mary and  Freddy particularly the scene where he's  coming out to you as bisexual and you  say no Freddy you're gay what intrigued  you the most about what you learned  about the two of them together and and  Mary and specifically I think it is that  kind of undefinable nature of the  relationship that has kind of infuriated  a lot of people in the way I've found  during the research that a lot of  Freddy's fans are very opinionated about  that relationship and I think it is that  undefinable quality to it that's quite  provocative that it doesn't fit into the  kind of genre or category of  relationship that were very that people  are more used to seeing at that time and  I think in a way that's what led to its  success in a way or at least was the  vehicle for its the longevity of that  relationship and it was just a  fascinating thing to explore I'm Brian  May was my main source of all things  Freddy and Mary he was actually the  person who introduced them  so to get his point of view kind of his  outside point of view off the trajectory  of their relationship starting from when  they first met and had this kind of  clarity of sight with one another that  so few were granted and so few got and  it was that clarity and that deep love  and respect that they found seemingly  very early on that was then able to  drive them and carry them throughout the  kind of unpredictable t